      "content": "Major criticisms of 15-minute cities include the potential to gentrify neighborhoods as high-density, walkable areas become desirable, often displacing long-term residents with lower incomes. Urban planners also argue that rigid adherence to short commute times may ignore the complex realities of existing infrastructure, making the initial implementation extremely difficult in sprawling regions. Furthermore, some experts worry this model could overemphasize proximity while underestimating the necessity of major inter-city travel for work, education, and leisure. Ultimately, critics contend that unless these concepts are adapted with flexible planning to avoid these pitfalls, they risk becoming exclusionous rather than inclusive solutions.",
